{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-adfe0e13621c8c06","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9014 — Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. Llama Stack is Tech\n13\tPreview today (corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/llama-stack.md`);\n14\tthe strategy graduates it to GA by closing quality gaps, committing to API\n15\tstability, establishing a production support posture, and promoting the\n16\tTrustyAI guardrails integration and Dashboard default availability.\n17\t\n18\tNo Staff Engineer Input section is present in the strategy.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n24\t| E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n25\t| E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n26\t| E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n27\t| E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n28\t| E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n29\t| E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n30\t\n31\tNotes on the epic list:\n32\t\n33\t- **Teams** are taken from the architecture context owning teams (RHOAI GenAI\n34\t  for Llama Stack, TrustyAI for TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard for Dashboard). The\n35\t  strategy itself has no Impacted Teams table (see Health Warnings).\n36\t- **E006 component** is the canonical `Documentation` component (from\n37\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`); its subject matter is Llama Stack GA\n38\t  documentation. E006's team is assumed to be the component owner (RHOAI\n39\t  GenAI) since the strategy names no docs team — flagged in Health Warnings.\n40\t- **E006 priority** is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy\n41\t  frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), even though HLR-6 is P2 — flagged in\n42\t  Health Warnings.\n43\t\n44\t## Dependency DAG\n45\t\n46\t```mermaid\n47\tgraph TD\n48\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Investigation, P0)\"]\n49\t    E002[\"E002 API stability guarantee (P0)\"]\n50\t    E003[\"E003 Production support posture (P0)\"]\n51\t    E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails integration (P1)\"]\n52\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard default enablement (P1)\"]\n53\t    E006[\"E006 GA documentation uplift (docs-authoring, P0)\"]\n54\t    E001 --> E002\n55\t    E001 --> E003\n56\t    E001 --> E004\n57\t    E002 --> E006\n58\t    E003 --> E006\n59\t    E004 --> E006\n60\t    E005 --> E006\n61\t```\n62\t\n63\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E006 (length 3).\n64\t\n65\t## DAG Justification\n66\t\n67\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n68\t|------|------|-----------|\n69\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The GA-readiness assessment produces the definitive GA scope; the size and content of the API stability work is an estimate until it lands (strategy Open Question 1). Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n70\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets are sized by the assessment's scale-limit and error-handling findings. Gate impact: `rewrite`. |\n71\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The assessment inventories gaps against the GA bar for the whole distribution; it may reveal additional guardrails-integration hardening beyond the known experimental-flag gap. Gate impact: `add_remediation` — the base integration work is defined by HLR-4 regardless. |\n72\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation must describe the published API versioning, deprecation policy, and tested upgrade path once built. |\n73\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation references the published support posture (SLOs, backport policy, supported configuration matrix). |\n74\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation covers the TrustyAI guardrails integration as a supported feature. |\n75\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | GA documentation (and TP migration notes) must reflect Dashboard default availability of Llama Stack management. |\n76\t\n77\t**Deliberate non-edges (parallel work):**\n78\t\n79\t| Non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n80\t|----------|------|-----------|\n81\t| E001/E002/E003 ∥ E005 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | The Dashboard change is fully specified by HLR-5 (flip the default, gate on entitlement) and does not build against any artifact the Llama Stack epics produce — per `dashboard.md`, the Dashboard talks to Kubernetes CRs only and has no private component APIs. The assessment's outcome does not change what the Dashboard epic does, so no gate applies (Step 3 decision rule). Release sequencing is flagged in Health Warnings, not as a DAG edge. |\n82\t| E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 15 / boundary axes | Same component and team but distinct logical changes with different work products (code/API hardening vs. support-process definition and configuration validation). No shared surface is built twice; they proceed concurrently. |\n83\t| E002/E003 ∥ E004 | Rules 13/14 (different components, repos, teams; no shared build artifact) | The TrustyAI integration work is in a different component and repo; it consumes the Llama Stack distribution as a running target, not as a build-against artifact produced by E002/E003. |\n84\t| E005 ∥ E004 | Rule 14 | Different repos and components; no shared artifacts. |\n85\t\n86\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n87\t\n88\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) | Notes |\n89\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|-------|\n90\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories TP→GA gaps, produces definitive GA scope | E001 | Direct mapping; the assessment is the epic's deliverable. |\n91\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Published API surface carries stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases | E002 | Direct mapping. |\n92\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: documented SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix | E003 | Direct mapping. Kept separate from E002: different logical change and work-product type (support-process definition vs. API hardening). |\n93\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails a supported, documented integration for GA distributions | E004 | Direct mapping; passive side (Llama Stack distribution accepting the integration) is captured as an acceptance criterion on E004 verifying against a GA distribution configuration. |\n94\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters | E005 | Direct mapping. |\n95\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards, including TP migration notes | E006 | Direct mapping; docs-authoring epic blocked by all Implementation epics per Rule 11. Priority P0 via docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Critical). |\n96\t\n97\tAll six HLRs are covered; none is bundled — each maps to exactly one epic.\n98\t\n99\t## Health Warnings\n100\t\n101\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n102\t   Teams table, no Dependencies table, no formal Given/When/Then acceptance\n103\t   criteria, and no Non-Functional Requirements section. Teams were derived\n104\t   from architecture context; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the\n105\t   HLRs and strategy problem statement.\n106\t2. **Docs epic priority divergence**: HLR-6 is P2, but the docs-authoring\n107\t   priority exception derives E006's priority from the strategy-level\n108\t   `priority: Critical` → P0. This is per-rule but diverges from the HLR's own\n109\t   priority; humans should confirm the docs uplift is actually scheduled at\n110\t   P0 urgency.\n111\t3. **Docs epic team assumption**: the strategy names no owning team for the\n112\t   documentation uplift. E006 is assigned to RHOAI GenAI (the Llama Stack\n113\t   owner); if a dedicated docs team owns GA content, reassign before\n114\t   submission.\n115\t4. **Release sequencing vs. build dependency for E005**: the Dashboard\n116\t   default-on flip has no DAG edge to the Llama Stack GA epics because it is\n117\t   build-independent, but shipping default-on before Llama Stack actually\n118\t   graduates would contradict the strategy's intent. Coordinate the release:\n119\t   default-on should land with (not before) the GA graduation.\n120\t5. **Scope trap watch (HLR-3)**: \"documented SLO targets\" implies measurement.\n121\t   If establishing the SLOs turns out to require new observability/telemetry\n122\t   infrastructure in Llama Stack, that is net-new engineering beyond\n123\t   documenting targets and should be re-scoped when the E001 assessment lands.\n124\t6. **Estimate uncertainty (strategy Open Question 1)**: until E001 lands, the\n125\t   size of E002/E003/E004 is an estimate — this is exactly why all three are\n126\t   gated on E001 rather than split further now. Premature splitting on an\n127\t   unvalidated estimate would produce artificial boundaries.\n128\t\n129\t## Tiered Delivery\n130\t\n131\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing (no upstream\n132\tPR/RFC or vendor coordination gates this strategy). The parallel structure is:\n133\tE005 (Dashboard default enablement) is fully independent Tier-1-style work\n134\tthat can proceed immediately; E002/E003/E004 are gated on the E001 assessment\n135\tresolving the GA scope; E006 (docs) is last by rule.\n136\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83248e61379c6dd8","agent_id":"a60b08dd582e62c70","sessio